===Earlier candidate speculation===
President of the National Council [[Wolfgang Sobotka]] of the ÖVP has ruled out running.<ref>{{cite web |date=9 February 2020 |title=Federal President: Sobotka does not want to run for office |url=https://orf.at/stories/3153687/ |website=[[ORF (broadcaster)|ORF]] |language=de |access-date=3 December 2021 |archive-date=3 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203094827/https://orf.at/stories/3153687/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

The SPÖ was previously undecided about whether they should run a candidate.<ref>{{cite web |date=30 November 2020 |title=Federal President: Too early to make a decision, say SPÖ state associations |url=https://orf.at/stories/3191821/ |website=[[ORF (broadcaster)|ORF]] |language=de |access-date=3 December 2021 |archive-date=3 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203094824/https://orf.at/stories/3191821/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In November 2020, federal chairwoman [[Pamela Rendi-Wagner]] stressed that a decision had not been made, but noted that the SPÖ has never challenged an incumbent president. She also ruled out running herself.<ref>{{cite web |date=13 December 2020 |title="Not in the life plan" Rendi-Wagner rules out Hofburg candidacy |url=https://www.kleinezeitung.at/politik/innenpolitik/5910944/ |website=[[Kleine Zeitung]] |language=de |access-date=3 December 2021 |archive-date=3 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203094829/https://www.kleinezeitung.at/politik/innenpolitik/5910944/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Second President of the National Council [[Doris Bures]] and Mayor of Vienna [[Michael Ludwig]] both stated that the party should support Van der Bellen if he chooses to run for a second term.<ref>{{cite web |date=28 November 2020 |title=Hofburg 2022: SPÖ is considering supporting Van der Bellen |url=https://kurier.at/politik/inland/hofburg-2022-spoe-ueberlegt-van-der-bellen-zu-unterstuetzen/401112618 |website=[[Kurier]] |language=de |access-date=3 December 2021 |archive-date=3 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203094822/https://kurier.at/politik/inland/hofburg-2022-spoe-ueberlegt-van-der-bellen-zu-unterstuetzen/401112618 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=27 March 2021 |title=Hofburg candidacy Ludwig: SPÖ should support Van der Bellen |url=https://www.kleinezeitung.at/politik/innenpolitik/5957920/ |website=[[Kleine Zeitung]] |language=de |access-date=3 December 2021 |archive-date=3 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203094822/https://www.kleinezeitung.at/politik/innenpolitik/5957920/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Tyrolean branch leader Georg Dornauer suggested that Bures herself should stand for election, even if Van der Bellen runs again.<ref>{{cite web |date=28 December 2020 |title=Dornauer for Bures as the SPÖ's Hofburg candidate |url=https://www.oe24.at/oesterreich/politik/dornauer-fuer-bures-als-hofburg-kandidatin-der-spoe/459151736 |website=[[Österreich (newspaper)|Österreich]] |language=de |access-date=3 December 2021 |archive-date=3 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203100324/https://www.oe24.at/oesterreich/politik/dornauer-fuer-bures-als-hofburg-kandidatin-der-spoe/459151736 |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Norbert Hofer]] of the [[Freedom Party of Austria]] (FPÖ), who was runner-up to Van der Bellen in 2016, has made conflicting statements about his intention to seek the presidency for a second time. In 2018, he stated that he plans to run in 2022.<ref>{{cite web |date=14 October 2018 |title=Hofer wants to run for president again in 2022 |url=https://www.derstandard.at/story/2000089321974/hofer-will-2022-noch-einmal-bundespraesident-werden |website=[[Der Standard]] |language=de |access-date=3 December 2021 |archive-date=3 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203094822/https://www.derstandard.at/story/2000089321974/hofer-will-2022-noch-einmal-bundespraesident-werden |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2020, he stated that he would not run if Van der Bellen sought re-election, but that he would if [[Rudolf Anschober]] was the Greens nominee.<ref>{{cite web |date=17 August 2020 |title=Hofer in the "summer talk": FPÖ will not challenge the Vienna election |url=https://orf.at/stories/3177814/ |website=[[ORF (broadcaster)|ORF]] |language=de |access-date=3 December 2021 |archive-date=3 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203094825/https://orf.at/stories/3177814/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Anschober himself subsequently ruled out running.<ref>{{cite web |date=21 August 2020 |title=Anschober does not want to become president and names four factors for corona traffic lights |url=https://www.derstandard.at/story/2000119518080/anschober-will-nicht-bundespraesident-werden-und-nennt-vier-faktoren-fuer |website=[[Der Standard]] |language=de |access-date=3 December 2021 |archive-date=3 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203094823/https://www.derstandard.at/story/2000119518080/anschober-will-nicht-bundespraesident-werden-und-nennt-vier-faktoren-fuer |url-status=live }}</ref> In February 2021, Hofer said it was "not unlikely" that he would run, and that he would make the decision a year in advance of the election.<ref>{{cite web |date=2 February 2021 |title=Hofer: "Not unlikely that I will compete" |url=https://www.krone.at/2332482 |website=[[Kronen Zeitung]] |language=de |access-date=3 December 2021 |archive-date=3 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203094823/https://www.krone.at/2332482 |url-status=live }}</ref> After Hofer's resignation as party leader in June, he stated he was unsure whether he would run for president. His successor [[Herbert Kickl]] voiced his confidence that Hofer would still run, however.<ref>{{cite web |date=9 June 2021 |title="I will stay 3rd President of the National Council" |url=https://www.puls24.at/news/politik/kickl-im-puls-24-interview-identitaere-seien-unterstuetzenswertes-projekt/236299 |website=Puls24.at |language=de |access-date=3 December 2021 |archive-date=3 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203100330/https://www.puls24.at/news/politik/kickl-im-puls-24-interview-identitaere-seien-unterstuetzenswertes-projekt/236299 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 13 May 2022, Hofer declared that he would not run for president in the election.<ref>{{cite web |date=13 May 2022 |title="Hofer will heuer nicht bei Hofburg-Wahl antreten" |url=https://orf.at/stories/3265619/ |website=orf.at |language=de |access-date=13 May 2022 |archive-date=13 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220513202806/https://orf.at/stories/3265619/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Campaign==
===Early campaign===
[[File:Wien - Hofburg, Leopoldinischer Trakt.JPG|thumb|right|200px|The official residence of the Austrian President since 1946: The ''Leopoldine Wing'' of the [[Hofburg#Leopoldine Wing|Hofburg]] in Vienna.]]
On 22 May 2022, President [[Alexander Van der Bellen]] announced his candidacy for re-election.<ref>{{cite web |date=22 May 2022 |title=Van der Bellen kandidiert erneut |url=https://orf.at/stories/3267238/ |website=orf.at |language=de |access-date=22 May 2022 |archive-date=22 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220522141939/https://orf.at/stories/3267238/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Opinion polls indicate high approval ratings for President Van der Bellen. A May 2022 survey showed that 63% of voters are satisfied or very satisfied with Van der Bellen's presidency.<ref>{{cite web |date=22 May 2022 |title=Wiederkandidatur: Van der Bellen startet mit 63 Prozent in den Wahlkampf |url=https://kurier.at/politik/inland/wiederkandidatur-van-der-bellen-startet-mit-63-prozent-in-den-wahlkampf/402016524 |website=kurier.at |language=de |access-date=22 May 2022 |archive-date=22 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220522143924/https://kurier.at/politik/inland/wiederkandidatur-van-der-bellen-startet-mit-63-prozent-in-den-wahlkampf/402016524 |url-status=live }}</ref> Another poll at the end of May had his approval rating at 59%, with 34% disapproving.<ref>{{cite web |date=30 May 2022 |title=Den Blauen ist Van der Bellen nicht grün genug |url=https://www.heute.at/s/den-blauen-ist-van-der-bellen-nicht-gruen-genug-100209477 |website=heute.at |language=de |access-date=11 June 2022 |archive-date=30 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530043707/https://www.heute.at/s/den-blauen-ist-van-der-bellen-nicht-gruen-genug-100209477 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The two major parties, the [[Austrian People's Party]] (ÖVP) and the [[Social Democratic Party of Austria]] (SPÖ), traditionally refrain from challenging a popular incumbent president if they choose to seek re-election. Following President Van der Bellen's announcement to run for re-election on 22 May 2022, the SPÖ announced that they will not run a candidate against him, instead they will actively endorse and support his campaign for re-election.<ref>{{cite web |date=22 May 2022 |title=Bundespräsident Van der Bellen tritt bei Wahl im Herbst erneut an |url=https://www.tt.com/artikel/30820993/spoe-will-bundespraesident-van-der-bellen-bei-wiederkandidatur-unterstuetzen |website=tt.com |language=de |access-date=22 May 2022 |archive-date=22 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220522132448/https://www.tt.com/artikel/30820993/spoe-will-bundespraesident-van-der-bellen-bei-wiederkandidatur-unterstuetzen |url-status=live }}</ref> 

On 23 May 2022, the governing party ÖVP announced that they will not run a candidate on their own but did not endorse the candidacy of Van der Bellen for re-election, instead wishing him "all the best". The Governor of Tyrol, Günther Platter, endorsed Van der Bellen.<ref>{{cite web |date=23 May 2022 |title=Nehammer: "Es wird keinen ÖVP-Kandidaten geben" |url=https://www.krone.at/2715521 |website=krone.at |language=de |access-date=23 May 2022 |archive-date=23 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220523171659/https://www.krone.at/2715521 |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[NEOS – The New Austria and Liberal Forum]] also announced on 22 May 2022 that they are endorsing and supporting Van der Bellen in his re-election effort.<ref>{{cite web |date=22 May 2022 |title=Van der Bellens zweite Kandidatur fix: So reagieren die Parteien |url=https://www.puls24.at/news/politik/van-der-bellens-zweite-kandidatur-fix-so-reagieren-die-parteien/265451 |website=puls24.at |language=de |access-date=22 May 2022 |archive-date=22 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220522153118/https://www.puls24.at/news/politik/van-der-bellens-zweite-kandidatur-fix-so-reagieren-die-parteien/265451 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

[[The Greens – The Green Alternative]], of which Van der Bellen was a long-time leader, also endorsed Van der Bellen after his announcement to run for re-election.<ref>{{cite web |date=22 May 2022 |title=Breite Unterstützung für Van der Bellen |url=https://orf.at/stories/3267307/ |website=orf.at |language=de |access-date=23 May 2022 |archive-date=23 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220523062411/https://orf.at/stories/3267307/ |url-status=live }}</ref> While both the SPÖ and NEOS did not support Van der Bellen financially, the Green Party did so with at least €500,000 in campaign money.

The opposition [[Freedom Party of Austria]] announced at an early stage that they will definitely run a candidate against Van der Bellen, while the new party [[MFG Austria – People Freedom Fundamental Rights]] said they are thinking about a candidacy against him. In June 2022, MFG party leader Michael Brunner announced his candidacy for president.

=== Intensive campaign period ===
[[File:P5650041 (52372790197).jpg|thumb|right|200px|President Alexander Van der Bellen at the United Nations in New York City on 20 September 2022, together with Austrian Chancellor Karl Nehammer and Austrian Foreign Minister Alexander Schallenberg]]
After the announcement of the seven official candidates on 7 September, the intensive campaign period started. President Alexander Van der Bellen (Green Party) decided not to participate in any TV debates with his challengers, citing that the "integrity of the office should not be harmed" and "his predecessors as President who ran for re-election also did not participate in TV debates". Some of Van der Bellen's opponents, such as Walter Rosenkranz (FPÖ) and Gerald Grosz (Independent, ex-FPÖ, ex-BZÖ) attacked him for his refusal to debate them and called him a "coward". 

President Van der Bellen instead focused on a billboard and Social Media campaign with slogans such as "The safe choice during stormy times." or "We are stronger together." or "You don't gamble with Austria." The President also used meetings with international leaders and visits to international organizations such as the United Nations in New York City to increase his profile ahead of the election and to appear statesmanlike.

Walter Rosenkranz also ran several billboard and Social Media campaigns with slogans like "Let's take back our freedoms." or "Uncompromisingly for Austria." or "Serving in the interest of the people, not the elites and powerful." The FPÖ also repeatedly mocked President Van der Bellen as "Van der [[Biden]]".

[[File:Marco Pogo in Graz.jpg|thumb|right|200px|Dominik Wlazny (alias Marco Pogo) campaigning in Graz on 23 September 2022]]
Dominik Wlazny (alias Marco Pogo) from the Beer Party launched a Social Media parody ad, mocking the campaign team of Alexander Van der Bellen, insinuating that the Van der Bellen camp is "afraid of Wlazny and his proposals." Later, Wlazny launched several billboards in which he prioritizes the fight against child poverty, violence against women, better pay for women, a rise in pensions, more renewable energy, better animal rights protection and a mandatory skills test for new cabinet members.

Gerald Grosz portrayed himself as a [[Donald Trump]]-style presidential candidate, wearing a red [[Make America Great Again|MAGA]] hat ("Make Austria Grosz Again"), with his last name Grosz resembling the German word "groß" for "great". Grosz attacked Van der Bellen and the Austrian ÖVP-Green government repeatedly for various failures on the [[Russian invasion of Ukraine]], the rising inflation and electricity prices etc. His main priority if elected President would be to dismiss the current Austrian government immediately, install a new cabinet of his own thinking, who would then call new parliamentary elections. His main campaign slogan is "Vote for Gerald Grosz and you get rid of Van der Bellen and the current government."

Like Rosenkranz and Grosz, Tassilo Wallentin and Michael Brunner also hail from the [[Radical right (Europe)|radical right]]. In his editorials for the ''[[Kronen Zeitung]]'' before becoming an official candidate, Wallentin repeatedly wrote that immigration in general and requests for asylum must be limited to zero, especially from Muslim-majority countries and warned that Muslims will be close to a majority in Austria in 20 years.

Brunner repeatedly warned of vaccination damages from COVID-19 vaccines and attacked the government on their policies during the pandemic. With the COVID-19 pandemic decreasing as a major topic during the campaign for president, Brunner followed the other candidates in focusing on rising inflation, electricity and gas prices. He said he favours the elimination of EU sanctions against Russia ("They are not working and harm the Austrian population.") and the full implementation of [[Declaration of Neutrality|Neutrality in Austria]] again, which in his opinion is currently not the case. He would also immediately dismiss the current Austrian government if elected president.

Heinrich Staudinger calls for poverty reduction, pacifism and the decrease in military spending and the focus of the military on disaster aid rather than engaging in conflicts. He strongly opposes increasing military spending to 2-3% of GDP like candidates Grosz and Rosenkranz. He strongly advocates help for immigrants and asylum seekers (his shoe manufacturing company employs numerous immigrants) and supports regional production methods with natural products and in line with the environment. He strongly supports measures to protect the environment and climate. He was vaccination-sceptic during the Covid pandemic. As President, he would make his first official state trip to the [[Democratic Republic of Congo]].

